 could have done  more in the past  to help diversity in this country max  you know we can't understate the fact  that aung san tsuchi she  is a nobel peace prize laureate she's  seen as an icon of peace  and yet she is here at the international  court of justice defending a regime  that's being accused of genocide  why would she take that risk  well first of all um the nobel the nobel  peace prize that she was awarded with uh  back in 1991 is it  is not in danger and the nobel peace  prize committee said last year had to  issue saw itself  forced to issue a statement that the  prize would be only awarded to people  would be awarded to people for their  acts in the past and that was against  the rules  to strip a nobel peace laureate off the  prize um  she is the foreign minister of myanmar  she's the de facto leader and she is  part of the regime herself and in her  eyes  this is an internal conflict and which  the west is now meddling in  and from what she said um there have be  there might have been problems with the  with with dealing with the insurgency in  the country but  her country is committed to the rule of  law and uh  wrongdoings will be brought to justice  and back home  um al sansoji has support of people  um right now from where i'm standing  right now i can actually see uh scores  of people standing out there with the  posters of  of their leader people who came from all  the way from from canada from  other countries in in europe uh here in  support uh uh  to to support their leader um so um this  may hurt your reputation on an  international level this case  but back home she does have the support  dw's correspondent  max sander reporting there for us from  the hague thank you very much  let's get more now on the alleged  atrocities that took place in rakhine  state and the way the hearing at the  hague is going down  in myanmar more than 700 thousand  rohingya muslims fled myanmar when the  military launched a crackdown in rakhine  state in augus